{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 17, :comments ["<b>And I will set My face.</b> 'My face' — I turn away from all My affairs to do harm to you." "<b>And those who hate you shall rule over you.</b> As it sounds — they will dominate you. The aggadah of Tanchuma expounds this passage." "<b>I in turn will do this.</b> I speak only in wrath, and likewise ״I in turn will walk with you in breach״ (verse 41)." "<b>And I will bring upon you.</b> That the blows would come upon you from one to the next — when the first is still visiting you, I will bring another and join it to it." "<b>Terror.</b> A blow that terrifies the creatures — and which is it? It is the plague of death." "<b>The consumption.</b> You find a person who is ill and bedridden but his flesh is intact on him — Scripture says ״שַׁחֶפֶת״, that he wastes away. Or sometimes he wastes away but rests comfortably and does not burn — Scripture says ״וְאֶת הַקַּדַּחַת״, teaching that he burns. Or sometimes he burns but thinks he will live — Scripture says ״מְכַלּוֹת עֵינַיִם״. Or he does not think he will live, but others think he will live — Scripture says ״וּמְדִיבֹת נָפֶשׁ״." "<b>And you shall sow your seed to no purpose.</b> You sow it and it does not sprout. If so, what — your enemies come and eat what? Scripture says ״וַאֲכָלֻהוּ אֹיְבֵיכֶם״. How so? The first year you sow and it does not sprout; the second year it sprouts, and enemies come and find produce for the days of the siege, while those inside die of famine because they gathered no produce the year before. Another interpretation: ״וּזְרַעְתֶּם לָרִיק זַרְעֲכֶם״ — the verse speaks of sons and daughters: you labor over them and raise them, and sin comes and consumes them, as it says ״אֲשֶׁר טִפַּחְתִּי וְרִבִּיתִי אֹיְבִי כִלָּם״ (Lamentations 2)." "<b>And I will set My face against you.</b> Just as it says of good ״וּפָנִיתִי אֲלֵיכֶם״, so it says of evil ״וְנָתַתִּי פָנַי״. They drew a parable: to a king who said to his servants, I turn from all my affairs and deal with you for evil." "<b>And you shall be smitten before your enemies.</b> That death kills you from inside, and your adversaries surround you from outside." "<b>And they that hate you shall rule over you.</b> That I set haters over you only from among you — for when the nations of the world stand against Israel, they seek only what is in the open, as it says ״וְהָיָה אִם זָרַע יִשְׂרָאֵל וְעָלָה מִדְיָן וַעֲמָלֵק וּבְנֵי קֶדֶם וגו׳ וַיַּחֲנוּ עֲלֵיהֶם וַיַּשְׁחִיתוּ אֶת יְבוּל הָאָרֶץ״ (Judges 6). But when I set over you haters from among you, they search after your hidden treasures, and so it says ״וַאֲשֶׁר אָכְלוּ שְׁאֵר עַמִּי וְעוֹרָם מֵעֲלֵיהֶם הִפְשִׁיטוּ וגו׳״ (Micah 3)." "<b>And you shall flee.</b> Out of terror." "<b>And none pursues you.</b> Without strength (Sifra)."], :book :leviticus, :voice :rashi, :lang :en, :chapter 26, :notes {}, :source-witnesses nil, :timestamp "2026-08-02T09:08:52.629193900Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}